data: {"type":"response.output_text.done","sequence_number":2151,"item_id":"msg_0471196a0420f5ec016b08546a4a5c87d187d49ccf60337727","output_index":1,"content_index":0,"text":"{\"queries\":[],\"result_json\":\"{\\\"reviews\\\":[{\\\"actor\\\":\\\"wren\\\",\\\"decision\\\":\\\"develop\\\",\\\"ability\\\":null,\\\"reason\\\":\\\"Develop a general bare-hand crossing-binding operation, not a Wren-specific recipe. The supplied searches and registry identify cord making, an overhand knot, a two-cord sheet bend, and a woven branch screen, but none attaches two crossing rigid pieces with cord. Their load paths, inputs and effects differ: tying cord tails or joining cords does not establish cord-to-branch contact, crossing retention or branch slip. Reuse those implementations where applicable rather than redeveloping cord physics. Missing branch/contact mechanisms belong in the current-conversation development handoff. Wren has not collected the two branches; neither learning nor development supplies them. The two reported 0.34 m cords are not assumed long enough, free of existing knots or joins, or currently available from incomplete item pages.\\\",\\\"specification\\\":\\\"General operation: hand-bind-crossing, parameterized by two exact owned rigid-piece item IDs, one exact owned cord ID, crossing pose, and mode attempt/inspect/trial/untie/cancel. Initial supported method is one enclosing wrap around a two-piece crossing, closed by an overhand knot in the same cord's ends. This is a minimal experimental binding, not a rated structural lashing. Modes share one work record and contact model; do not introduce separate assessment or diagnostic abilities. Support applicable materials through registered material laws; initially use represented branch pieces and hand-twisted fiber cord. Unsupported material mechanics receive a bounded observed fit/handling precursor and a development dependency, never an invented successful binding.\\\\nPrerequisites: individually completed study; exact owned, reachable, sound enough, unreserved separate pieces; an available finished cord with accessible ends and no conflicting knot/join; dry stable footing, clear hand/work space, and feasible bare-hand positioning. No assumed tools, anchors, extra people, stock, extra cord or automatic collection. A branch-screen component is not a loose branch. Wren's item-226-wren-2532 and item-250-wren-2847 are candidate cord arguments only after authoritative current-state validation; retained join-260-wren-2978 knowledge does not establish their present availability. If joined or knotted, use the applicable existing untying operation first. One cord is sufficient as an interface input; using both as an extended assembly requires a real existing hand-join-fiber-cords result and supported routing through that join, otherwise this minimal version refuses that argument without charge.\\\\nCosts: study 4 actual rounds at 0.2 energy each, separately for every learner. Attempt reserves the three inputs and takes four paid work rounds, 0.5 energy each: measure/position; route enclosing wrap; form and tuck closure; dress and release-check. Each completed stage persists its actual geometry and measurements. Inspect costs one round and 0.2 energy; bounded trial one round and 0.5 energy, accepting force greater than 0 and at most 10 N and duration greater than 0 and at most 60 s; untie takes two rounds at 0.5 energy each, withdrawing the closure end then opening/removing the wrap. Cancel is immediate and free. No automatic work during review, study waiting, or an unworked round. Materials are retained components rather than consumable recipe units; charge actual material damage, not a fictitious stock cost.\\\\nImplementation dependencies and reuse: record source/version dependencies on hand-twist-fiber-cord and its fiber-cord@1 geometry, strength, creep and fragment handling; hand-tie-cord-knot and cord-knot@1 bend limits, overhand paths, dressing, damage and jamming; and hand-join-fiber-cords/cord-bend@1 only when validating an existing join. Record extracted shared code and modifications explicitly. Branch-screen supplies no fastening or strength law; do not transplant its windbreak effect. Add a versioned crossing-contact law in the existing development handoff: observable piece dimensions and condition, contact paths, collision clearance, available hand grips, routing feasibility, wrap tension, frictional slip and release behavior. Existing reed friction assumptions may be reused only for their applicable contact pair; cord-to-branch friction and branch bending/break response require an explicit supported law or actual bounded measurement. They are not inferred from cord-to-cord friction.\\\\nExecution: obtain represented or physically measured local dimensions and branch condition through paid handling; do not invent dimensions from the generic word branch. Store piece transforms, crossing angle, contact locations and accessible ends. Compute the wrap centerline around the actual crossing with material bend limits and hand/passage clearance. Cord centerline equals routed wrap plus closure path plus both tails and any remaining slack; it may not exceed the available measured centerline. Use cord-knot@1 closure constraints, including pinch and retained-tail allowances; do not merely subtract two branch circumferences or assume a 0.34 m fit. Each stage checks whether one person can hold and route the pieces with the hands actually available. No invisible third hand or fixed anchor. An infeasible route or insufficient length produces a paid, measured shortfall at the stage where discovered and no completed binding.\\\\nDressing and releasing must execute supported closure retention and contact mechanics. A released wrap that opens, loosens or lets either branch escape is failed or loose, not successfully bound. If retained, create a relation referencing the original three item IDs and their geometry, without copying items, quantities, ownership or cord length. Installed pieces remain conserved components and cannot simultaneously be consumed, separately carried away or assigned to another work. The relation moves only as the actual assembly permits and counts the original material load once. It grants no shelter, usable anchor, furniture, strength rating, health, warmth or comfort effect.\\\\nA trial needs a real opposed hand-grip pair on the pieces, clear of the crossing; one hand must not be silently replaced by a fixture. Specify grip sites and force direction. Apply only the minimum of requested force, existing actor/grip limits, and any conservative supported test limit; record the actual force history and duration, not merely requested values. Resolve cord extension/creep, closure behavior, contact slip, relative piece motion and material damage using the registered laws, stopping on loss of grip, slip, breakage or unsafe geometry. Refuse unsupported load modes rather than declaring a pass. A held result means only that particular arrangement, force and time. Inspection reports visible routing, closure, slack, displacement and damage, not hidden strength. Untying can fail or jam under cord-knot@1; no forced restoration to pristine cord.\\\\nConservation and lifecycle: preserve item IDs, material amounts, origin, existing wear and cord centerline through all stages. Breakage produces conserved fragments or damaged piece records, never replacements. During partial work keep only genuine supported geometry; anything needing the actor's continuing grip relaxes on interruption. Low energy, leaving reach, changed footing, conflicting use or missing clearance pauses before charging the next stage. Resume by workId revalidates state and continues from the last physically surviving stage; paid history remains even if a relaxed route must be redone. Cancel releases unused reservations once and leaves worked material and actual retained geometry where they are. A retained completed binding remains a relation, not a permanently reserved job. Death stops work, removes hand support, resolves relaxation and leaves materials and any retained relation at the location under ordinary ownership/death rules. No paid time, energy, wear or damage is refunded; unused material is released, not recreated. Completed untying removes the relation and releases the same components.\\\\nOngoing behavior and maintenance: no continuing hand tension after release. Apply existing ageing and environmental wear once to the original components. Actual subsequent movement, handling or loading must resolve through the crossing-contact law and update slack, slip and retention; unsupported loads have no certified holding effect. Loss of retention immediately changes the relation to loose or separated and removes any constrained motion. Inspection does not repair anything. Maintenance in this version consists of inspection, an actual untying attempt, and a fresh paid binding attempt using available components; no automatic tightening, healing or free replacement. Until the contact model and release check are executable, expose only paid fit/routing observations, leave the work explicitly incomplete, and retain the missing mechanism in the same development handoff rather than claiming completion.\\\",\\\"learning\\\":4,\\\"experienceReview\\\":\\\"Evaluate information and participation separately from physical holding. Requesting, naming, registering, studying, waiting and becoming capable produce no experiential success reward. A paid measurement, informative fit failure, observed slip, or genuinely observed held trial may produce a verified personal finding with exact item/work/event references and measured conditions. Repeated identical findings are confirmations, not new information; deduplicate across probe, knot, cord-join and this operation by functional observation rather than ability name. This version grants no automatic short pleasure or satisfaction: both added amounts and caps are zero, so no new cooldown or maintenance reward process is introduced. Any future nonzero experience effect needs an explicit bounded, decaying, personally applicable rule in the existing engine, including modest familiar-practice eligibility; it is not a reason to rerun model review. No direct writes to protected reward accounts, budget, goals or permissions. Nutrition is unchanged and physical comfort gain is zero, within the global cap of 10. No other participant or their consent, communication, satisfaction or action is inferred.\\\",\\\"visualReview\\\":\\\"Emit observable stage-start/completion, measured-shortfall, wrap-routed, closure-tucked, release-held, release-loosened, test-start/stopped, grip-slip, crossing-slip, damage/break, pause/resume/cancel and untie-result events only when those actions occur. Provide reusable versioned metadata with work/relation ID, component IDs, actual piece transforms, crossing/contact points, cord centerline route and remaining lengths, closure crossings, visible condition, supported grip positions, stage, retention state and measured trial force/duration. Reuse existing fiber-cord, cord-knot and fragment visual components with recorded code provenance. Any schematic rendering is labeled schematic and conveys only measured or simulated supported geometry, not a strength claim. Render partial wrapping, looseness, displaced pieces, jammed closure and damage distinctly. Failed, interrupted or unimplemented contact/release stages cannot display a completed secure binding, successful trial badge, shelter overlay or strength rating. Learning alone creates no object or success event.\\\"}]}\"}","logprobs":[]}

